1
00:00:07,337 --> 00:00:09,339
[WOMAN 10A screams]

2
00:00:13,510 --> 00:00:15,888
Yeah. Very dead.

3
00:00:15,930 --> 00:00:17,264
Have a look.

4
00:00:18,724 --> 00:00:20,392
It was quite clearly a murder.

5
00:00:20,434 --> 00:00:21,685
Murder?

6
00:00:24,313 --> 00:00:26,064
So, William,

7
00:00:26,106 --> 00:00:28,943
if you don't have any pressing
matters before we reach London,

8
00:00:28,984 --> 00:00:30,319
how about a friendly competition

9
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:32,488
to see which of us
finds the culprit first?

10
00:00:32,529 --> 00:00:34,782
[LESTRADE]
Huh? You're not serious!

11
00:00:34,824 --> 00:00:36,533
This is a murder,
not one of your games!

12
00:00:36,575 --> 00:00:37,868
I accept.

13
00:00:37,910 --> 00:00:40,412
No doubt it'll prove
entertaining.

14
00:00:40,454 --> 00:00:41,705
However,

15
00:00:41,747 --> 00:00:44,124
this case seems more complicated
than at first glance.

16
00:00:44,166 --> 00:00:45,918
[GROUP gasps]

17
00:00:55,051 --> 00:00:56,846
[HOLMES]
John.

18
00:00:59,097 --> 00:01:00,307
[TRANSPORT POLICE 11A]
All right, then.

19
00:01:00,349 --> 00:01:02,852
What's all the fuss?

20
00:01:02,893 --> 00:01:04,812
[LESTRADE]
The transport police.

21
00:02:37,571 --> 00:02:41,075
[gasps] What's the
meaning of this?

22
00:02:41,116 --> 00:02:44,536
Right then, we're in charge
of this investigation!

23
00:02:44,578 --> 00:02:45,913
Arrest this man!

24
00:02:45,955 --> 00:02:47,163
[TRANSPORT POLICE 11B]
Aye!

25
00:02:47,205 --> 00:02:48,916
[gasps] Wait a moment!

26
00:02:48,958 --> 00:02:50,042
This blood.

27
00:02:50,084 --> 00:02:52,669
It's from when someone
ran into me at the loo!

28
00:02:58,092 --> 00:02:59,885
[WATSON]
I went back to my compartment,

29
00:02:59,927 --> 00:03:02,178
and after a short while,
I noticed the stain.

30
00:03:02,220 --> 00:03:03,513
Please believe me!

31
00:03:03,555 --> 00:03:05,599
[TRANSPORT POLICE 11B] We'll
discuss it at headquarters.

32
00:03:05,641 --> 00:03:06,266
Now, come along!

33
00:03:06,308 --> 00:03:07,684
[LESTRADE]
Just a moment.

34
00:03:07,726 --> 00:03:10,520
I am Inspector Lestrade
of Scotland Yard.

35
00:03:10,562 --> 00:03:13,065
Oh. Their only
competent detective!

36
00:03:13,107 --> 00:03:15,651
Right, uh... Anyhow,

37
00:03:15,692 --> 00:03:18,028
this is consulting
detective Sherlock Holmes.

38
00:03:18,070 --> 00:03:19,738
[TRANSPORT POLICE 11A]
In the flesh?

39
00:03:19,780 --> 00:03:22,574
[LESTRADE] And the man whom
you've cast your suspicion upon,

40
00:03:22,616 --> 00:03:25,285
is his friend,
Doctor John H. Watson.

41
00:03:25,327 --> 00:03:26,996
[TRANSPORT POLICE 11A]
I had no idea.

42
00:03:28,455 --> 00:03:30,290
Doctor Watson is not
the sort of man

43
00:03:30,332 --> 00:03:32,001
who would commit
such a heinous crime.

44
00:03:32,042 --> 00:03:34,586
I implore you to exercise
your best judgement.

45
00:03:34,628 --> 00:03:36,755
Yes, but under the
circumstances--

46
00:03:36,797 --> 00:03:40,009
[HOLMES grunts] This is
a waste of precious time!

47
00:03:40,050 --> 00:03:42,218
You're the ones who don't
understand the circumstances,

48
00:03:42,260 --> 00:03:43,679
you bollocks-for-brains!

49
00:03:43,720 --> 00:03:44,930
[TRANSPORT POLICE 11B gasps]

50
00:03:44,972 --> 00:03:46,682
[TRANSPORT POLICE 11A]
Sir! What do you mean by that?

51
00:03:46,723 --> 00:03:48,934
Wake up, man! As we speak,

52
00:03:48,976 --> 00:03:51,061
the killer's still on the train!

53
00:03:51,103 --> 00:03:53,147
Unless he's leapt from
a locomotive traveling

54
00:03:53,188 --> 00:03:56,358
to London at a hundred
kilometres per hour!

55
00:03:56,399 --> 00:03:59,277
The next station this train
stops at after leaving Doncaster

56
00:03:59,319 --> 00:04:00,154
will be Grantham.

57
00:04:00,195 --> 00:04:02,739
That's roughly 80
kilometers away.

58
00:04:02,781 --> 00:04:03,532
Which leaves--

59
00:04:03,573 --> 00:04:05,117
[WILLIAM]
Roughly 48 minutes.

60
00:04:05,159 --> 00:04:06,576
[HOLMES]
Precisely!

61
00:04:06,618 --> 00:04:09,746
If we don't discover the real
murderer by then, he'll escape!

62
00:04:09,788 --> 00:04:13,125
Can the Transport
Police find them? Can they?

63
00:04:13,167 --> 00:04:15,961
Well, we'll do our best...

64
00:04:16,003 --> 00:04:18,338
[HOLMES grunts, chuckles]

65
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:21,216
Why not put us in charge?

66
00:04:21,257 --> 00:04:24,260
Holmes has actually
solved a good number of cases.

67
00:04:24,302 --> 00:04:27,139
What do you say we leave
the investigation up to him?

68
00:04:27,181 --> 00:04:28,182
[TRANSPORT POLICE 11A]
Inspector!

69
00:04:28,223 --> 00:04:30,350
I don't care how famous
a detective he is.

70
00:04:30,392 --> 00:04:32,561
Brother, I'm confused.

71
00:04:32,602 --> 00:04:35,064
We had nothing to do
with this incident.

72
00:04:35,105 --> 00:04:37,107
Then why get entangled
with Holmes?

73
00:04:37,149 --> 00:04:38,316
We shouldn't.

74
00:04:38,358 --> 00:04:39,943
That said,

75
00:04:39,985 --> 00:04:45,574
an innocent man has fallen
under suspicion of murder.

76
00:04:45,615 --> 00:04:48,702
Finding the real culprit,
and proving their guilt,

77
00:04:48,744 --> 00:04:52,122
would prove impossible
for the transport police.

78
00:04:52,164 --> 00:04:57,920
So naturally, we have to fulfill
our noblesse oblige, agreed?

79
00:04:57,961 --> 00:05:01,215
Very well then. We'll help.

80
00:05:01,256 --> 00:05:02,340
[LESTRADE,
TRANSPORT POLICE 11A gasp]

81
00:05:02,382 --> 00:05:05,094
I'm telling you,
all we require is 48 minutes.

82
00:05:05,135 --> 00:05:07,512
We'll have the whole case
figured out by then!

83
00:05:07,554 --> 00:05:09,556
Isn't that right, Liam?

84
00:05:09,598 --> 00:05:12,017
[LOUIS]
Liam? Rather chummy.

85
00:05:12,059 --> 00:05:13,351
What are they? Friends?

86
00:05:13,393 --> 00:05:14,770
Yes.

87
00:05:14,811 --> 00:05:16,939
In fact, I suspect we won't even
need the full amount of time

88
00:05:16,980 --> 00:05:18,232
to reach our findings.

89
00:05:18,273 --> 00:05:21,193
Ah. Well, listen to you.

90
00:05:21,235 --> 00:05:24,029
Athough this is little more
than a chance encounter,

91
00:05:24,071 --> 00:05:26,448
I refuse to bring shame
to the Moriarty name

92
00:05:26,489 --> 00:05:28,033
by standing idly by.

93
00:05:28,075 --> 00:05:30,452
That sounds good to me!
We've a contest then.

94
00:05:30,493 --> 00:05:32,287
Who can unmask
the culprit first.

95
00:05:32,328 --> 00:05:34,831
Still treating this
like a damn game.

96
00:05:34,873 --> 00:05:35,999
I'll allow it.

97
00:05:36,041 --> 00:05:39,002
Only because Inspector
Lestrade has faith in you.

98
00:05:39,044 --> 00:05:41,088
You'll have until
we reach the next station.

99
00:05:41,130 --> 00:05:42,589
Much obliged.

100
00:05:42,631 --> 00:05:44,507
However, we're
placing Doctor Watson

101
00:05:44,549 --> 00:05:48,887
in our custody until any
new evidence comes to light.

102
00:05:48,929 --> 00:05:50,472
Sherlock...

103
00:05:58,105 --> 00:06:00,983
Let's be quick.
Time is of the essence.

104
00:06:01,024 --> 00:06:03,110
You have read my mind.

105
00:06:06,571 --> 00:06:09,074
Hey, wait, you don't have a key!

106
00:06:09,116 --> 00:06:11,034
So how'd you open the door then?

107
00:06:11,076 --> 00:06:12,535
Hah. Some policeman.

108
00:06:12,577 --> 00:06:14,704
You mean you can't
even pick a lock?

109
00:06:34,975 --> 00:06:37,393
So that's it. Just
about done here.

110
00:06:37,435 --> 00:06:38,645
Have you found something that

111
00:06:38,687 --> 00:06:40,647
will lead us to
the murderer, Holmes?

112
00:06:40,689 --> 00:06:42,024
[HOLMES]
Yeah.

113
00:06:42,065 --> 00:06:46,653
Here's what we know: The victim
is a jeweller from London.

114
00:06:46,695 --> 00:06:49,072
Trevor Redwood.

115
00:06:49,114 --> 00:06:51,908
The killer is male.

116
00:06:51,950 --> 00:06:55,537
He boarded the train at
the terminus in Durham.

117
00:06:55,578 --> 00:06:58,498
When the drugging slash
robbery went awry,

118
00:06:58,540 --> 00:07:00,583
he knifed the victim.

119
00:07:03,337 --> 00:07:05,130
If Redwood was drugged,

120
00:07:05,172 --> 00:07:08,091
then that must mean murder
wasn't the sole motive!

121
00:07:09,509 --> 00:07:11,053
Unmistakably.

122
00:07:11,094 --> 00:07:12,888
Obviously.

123
00:07:12,929 --> 00:07:14,430
[gasps, grunts]

124
00:07:14,472 --> 00:07:16,933
[LESTRADE] As if one
Holmes wasn't enough!

125
00:07:16,975 --> 00:07:20,937
Now there's two of them
with this Moriarty fellow.

126
00:07:21,855 --> 00:07:25,483
I give. If you wouldn't
mind explaining...

127
00:07:25,525 --> 00:07:27,569
[HOLMES]
Very well, as a favour.

128
00:07:27,610 --> 00:07:30,780
Time is short, so I shall
only run through this once.

129
00:07:30,822 --> 00:07:33,325
First off, the carpet
in this compartment

130
00:07:33,367 --> 00:07:35,744
has two sets of
men's footprints.

131
00:07:35,785 --> 00:07:37,579
One set belongs to the victim.

132
00:07:37,620 --> 00:07:41,624
The other is from the Durham
man, measuring ten inches.

133
00:07:41,666 --> 00:07:43,835
What makes you say Durham?

134
00:07:43,877 --> 00:07:48,673
[HOLMES] Why not ask a certain
professor who resides there?

135
00:07:48,715 --> 00:07:49,841
[LESTRADE]
They're muddied.

136
00:07:49,883 --> 00:07:52,844
Indeed. It was
raining in Durham.

137
00:07:52,886 --> 00:07:54,888
However, since our
first stop in York,

138
00:07:54,930 --> 00:07:56,556
the weather has been clear.

139
00:07:56,598 --> 00:07:59,935
Now I see. So we've narrowed
down the murderer's sex

140
00:07:59,976 --> 00:08:01,978
and the station they boarded.

141
00:08:05,190 --> 00:08:08,484
Additionally, this is what
Holmes was referring to.

142
00:08:08,526 --> 00:08:09,945
If you'll observe.

143
00:08:09,986 --> 00:08:13,448
Whisky... with a hint of more.

144
00:08:13,489 --> 00:08:16,952
There is still the faint,
lingering aroma of chloral.

145
00:08:16,993 --> 00:08:19,621
When mixed with alcohol,
it becomes a dangerous drug

146
00:08:19,662 --> 00:08:22,791
which exhibits powerful
soporific effects.

147
00:08:22,832 --> 00:08:24,500
Whatever means were used,

148
00:08:24,542 --> 00:08:27,670
somebody had to have laced
the drink with the compound.

149
00:08:27,712 --> 00:08:29,381
But why would they
drug the victim

150
00:08:29,423 --> 00:08:31,007
before brutally stabbing them?

151
00:08:31,049 --> 00:08:32,467
[HOLMES groans]

152
00:08:32,508 --> 00:08:35,137
Because something happened which
the killer was not expecting.

153
00:08:35,178 --> 00:08:37,013
Nothing more.

154
00:08:37,055 --> 00:08:40,809
Without a doubt,
this was an unplanned murder.

155
00:08:40,850 --> 00:08:43,312
If killing this man
was the true objective,

156
00:08:43,353 --> 00:08:45,355
there would be no reason
to do so on a train

157
00:08:45,397 --> 00:08:48,191
where escape would
be impossible.

158
00:08:48,233 --> 00:08:51,194
More than likely,
the culprit drugged the victim

159
00:08:51,236 --> 00:08:54,323
and attempted to rob him
whilst he was incapacitated.

160
00:08:57,075 --> 00:08:59,202
[WILLIAM]
Either the chloral was too weak

161
00:08:59,244 --> 00:09:01,204
or his tolerance too high.

162
00:09:01,246 --> 00:09:04,707
Regardless, he awoke
during the theft.

163
00:09:04,749 --> 00:09:07,669
Resulting in an unexpected
surprise for both.

164
00:09:08,628 --> 00:09:10,797
[WILLIAM] Judging from
the shape of his wound

165
00:09:10,839 --> 00:09:14,592
the weapon was a keen blade,
a knife of some sort.

166
00:09:14,634 --> 00:09:16,761
And from the marks
left on the case,

167
00:09:16,803 --> 00:09:20,056
the killer tried prying
it open with said knife.

168
00:09:20,098 --> 00:09:23,185
[LESTRADE]
A knife, huh? I don't see one.

169
00:09:23,226 --> 00:09:25,187
The murderer must
have left with it.

170
00:09:25,228 --> 00:09:27,272
[WILLIAM]
The chances are quite high.

171
00:09:27,314 --> 00:09:29,774
When the victim awakened,
a struggle ensued

172
00:09:29,816 --> 00:09:32,902
during which
Redwood was stabbed.

173
00:09:32,944 --> 00:09:35,447
The amount of blood must
have surprised the killer.

174
00:09:35,489 --> 00:09:37,449
There are indications
he held his hand over

175
00:09:37,491 --> 00:09:39,117
the wound to stop the bleeding.

176
00:09:39,159 --> 00:09:40,327
[LESTRADE]
Quite so.

177
00:09:40,369 --> 00:09:42,745
Despite there being no blood
on the victim's hands,

178
00:09:42,787 --> 00:09:44,914
a handprint was left behind

179
00:09:44,956 --> 00:09:46,500
It stands to reason he panicked

180
00:09:46,541 --> 00:09:48,877
and forgot the
burglary all together.

181
00:09:48,918 --> 00:09:50,962
Didn't attempt to hide the body.

182
00:09:51,004 --> 00:09:55,258
Not to mention, he left behind
the whisky and the briefcase.

183
00:09:55,300 --> 00:09:57,844
The knife and key are
the only things missing.

184
00:09:57,886 --> 00:09:59,304
All the culprit
could think to do

185
00:09:59,346 --> 00:10:02,182
was leave the room
and lock the door.

186
00:10:02,224 --> 00:10:04,767
The crime scene indicates
that the culprit was flustered

187
00:10:04,809 --> 00:10:06,228
by what transpired.

188
00:10:06,269 --> 00:10:08,355
[HOLMES]
I'm in full agreement.

189
00:10:15,570 --> 00:10:17,406
You're finished with
the crime scene?

190
00:10:17,447 --> 00:10:19,866
Indeed. We have
all that we need.

191
00:10:19,908 --> 00:10:21,659
Let's go, John--

192
00:10:25,747 --> 00:10:27,874
Thirty-nine minutes remain.

193
00:10:29,584 --> 00:10:31,086
[HOLMES]
Hm.

194
00:10:31,127 --> 00:10:32,212
Lord Moriarty!

195
00:10:32,254 --> 00:10:34,672
I brought the passenger list
for you to look at

196
00:10:34,714 --> 00:10:36,841
along with each of the
compartment layouts!

197
00:10:36,883 --> 00:10:37,926
Wonderful.

198
00:10:37,967 --> 00:10:40,762
But first I have a few
questions to ask you.

199
00:10:40,803 --> 00:10:41,846
[CREWMAN 11A]
Hm?

200
00:10:43,973 --> 00:10:45,350
[LOUIS]
Holmes.

201
00:10:45,392 --> 00:10:50,021
The one man in the world to whom
my brother pays due respect.

202
00:10:50,063 --> 00:10:52,607
However, from
William's standpoint,

203
00:10:52,648 --> 00:10:54,734
he's nothing more than a pawn.

204
00:10:54,776 --> 00:10:59,364
And yet, the man addresses him
so casually, not to mention,

205
00:10:59,406 --> 00:11:01,824
he dares to challenge
him to a contest.

206
00:11:01,866 --> 00:11:04,077
Could he be any cheekier?

207
00:11:04,119 --> 00:11:07,038
The moment he stops being
useful to us, I swear...

208
00:11:07,080 --> 00:11:08,582
[WILLIAM]
Interesting.

209
00:11:09,541 --> 00:11:12,377
Allow me to thank you.
You've been helpful.

210
00:11:12,419 --> 00:11:15,213
[CREWMAN 11A] No need, my lord,
the pleasure was mine.

211
00:11:17,132 --> 00:11:19,008
It was an honor, sir.

212
00:11:21,470 --> 00:11:23,138
Have you found anything new?

213
00:11:23,179 --> 00:11:25,140
Indeed.

214
00:11:25,181 --> 00:11:28,310
Holmes will narrow down
who the culprit might be

215
00:11:28,351 --> 00:11:30,812
by relying on
circumstantial evidence.

216
00:11:30,853 --> 00:11:32,606
Who could have
gotten him to drink,

217
00:11:32,647 --> 00:11:35,150
who could've left
the footprints.

218
00:11:35,191 --> 00:11:36,318
As for me,

219
00:11:36,359 --> 00:11:39,904
I have a different approach--
the psychological.

220
00:11:39,946 --> 00:11:43,116
I'll look for clues to
profile the killer's mind.

221
00:11:54,836 --> 00:11:58,047
[WILLIAM] As we've established,
the culprit was greatly alarmed

222
00:11:58,089 --> 00:12:00,383
when the crime
didn't go as planned.

223
00:12:03,761 --> 00:12:06,222
But he still managed
to lock the door as he left

224
00:12:06,264 --> 00:12:09,725
out of fear of someone walking
by and discovering his crime.

225
00:12:09,767 --> 00:12:11,019
Then at the lavatory,

226
00:12:11,060 --> 00:12:13,938
the culprit stumbled
into Mister Watson.

227
00:12:13,980 --> 00:12:15,315
Louis.

228
00:12:15,357 --> 00:12:17,942
Why do you think the killer
went there in the first place?

229
00:12:20,945 --> 00:12:24,616
They would be carrying the knife
and room key at that point.

230
00:12:24,658 --> 00:12:25,908
If found with either,

231
00:12:25,950 --> 00:12:29,412
they would be declaring
themselves the murderer.

232
00:12:29,454 --> 00:12:32,582
So he must have gone there
to dispose of the evidence!

233
00:12:32,624 --> 00:12:35,293
[WILLIAM]
Exactly. That's one reason.

234
00:12:35,335 --> 00:12:38,296
The windows in the passengers
compartments don't open.

235
00:12:38,338 --> 00:12:41,341
So he needed to throw
them from the washroom.

236
00:12:41,383 --> 00:12:44,802
Once the tracks are searched,
no doubt both will be recovered.

237
00:12:44,844 --> 00:12:47,430
[LOUIS]
And? What was the other reason?

238
00:12:47,472 --> 00:12:50,392
[WILLIAM]
It's quite simple, really.

239
00:12:50,433 --> 00:12:53,478
He wanted to wash the
blood off his clothes.

240
00:12:53,520 --> 00:12:57,106
[LOUIS]
Yes. The victim's blood.

241
00:12:57,148 --> 00:12:58,483
But if he tossed
out his clothing

242
00:12:58,525 --> 00:13:01,653
along with the weapon
and key, then-- [gasps]

243
00:13:01,695 --> 00:13:04,822
Right, he couldn't
dispose of his clothes,

244
00:13:04,864 --> 00:13:06,324
otherwise he would
have stood out.

245
00:13:06,366 --> 00:13:09,785
Therefore, we can assume
that he's still wearing them.

246
00:13:09,827 --> 00:13:12,997
He had no other choice
but to wash them.

247
00:13:13,039 --> 00:13:16,459
Holmes determined the killer was
male based on the footprints.

248
00:13:16,501 --> 00:13:19,837
But he didn't narrow
it down further than that.

249
00:13:19,879 --> 00:13:21,172
Rest assured,

250
00:13:21,214 --> 00:13:25,760
soon he will realise the tracks
can be found everywhere.

251
00:13:25,801 --> 00:13:30,098
Despite the difference in size,
the footprints remain identical.

252
00:13:30,139 --> 00:13:32,308
All from one source.

253
00:13:34,810 --> 00:13:37,939
[HOLMES] The shoes are
issued to the crewmen.

254
00:13:41,817 --> 00:13:44,112
Of course. It makes sense.

255
00:13:44,153 --> 00:13:47,073
A crewman would offer a
first-class passenger a drink.

256
00:13:47,115 --> 00:13:49,618
And they couldn't get
rid of their jacket.

257
00:13:49,659 --> 00:13:51,745
Let's not forget his gloves.

258
00:13:52,787 --> 00:13:56,541
[WILLIAM] Each pair is white
and individually monogrammed.

259
00:13:56,583 --> 00:13:59,919
Meaning, if he disposed
of his jacket and gloves,

260
00:13:59,961 --> 00:14:03,256
he'd essentially be
confessing to the murder.

261
00:14:03,298 --> 00:14:05,341
And after the killer went
to the washroom to clean

262
00:14:05,383 --> 00:14:07,802
the blood off of his uniform,

263
00:14:07,843 --> 00:14:10,930
he ended up bumping
right into Mister Watson.

264
00:14:10,972 --> 00:14:13,516
Should we search for
the guilty party now?

265
00:14:16,394 --> 00:14:19,188
There are six crewmen
aboard this train.

266
00:14:19,230 --> 00:14:21,399
Once Holmes reaches
this conclusion,

267
00:14:21,441 --> 00:14:23,276
he will gather them up for us.

268
00:14:23,318 --> 00:14:29,532
Before he does, though, I will
need to meet each individually.

269
00:14:29,574 --> 00:14:31,534
Just to be sure.

270
00:14:47,216 --> 00:14:50,094
There it is an
outline of the crime

271
00:14:50,136 --> 00:14:51,846
as Liam and I see it.

272
00:14:51,887 --> 00:14:53,431
[TRANSPORT POLICE 11A]
And us as well.

273
00:14:53,473 --> 00:14:55,809
If he couldn't throw out
the blood-stained clothes.

274
00:14:55,850 --> 00:14:58,269
He would be left with no
conceivable option other than

275
00:14:58,311 --> 00:15:00,396
washing them off
in the loo's sink.

276
00:15:00,438 --> 00:15:02,982
Yeah. Which means

277
00:15:03,024 --> 00:15:05,443
the killer is
one of the crewmen.

278
00:15:05,485 --> 00:15:07,862
That's our conclusion.

279
00:15:07,903 --> 00:15:10,490
Both of you with a
ten-inch shoe size,

280
00:15:10,532 --> 00:15:12,158
please step forward.

281
00:15:14,410 --> 00:15:16,412
Now, if you gentlemen
wouldn't mind

282
00:15:16,454 --> 00:15:18,206
letting us look at your gloves.

283
00:15:18,247 --> 00:15:20,208
But why our gloves, sir?

284
00:15:20,249 --> 00:15:21,793
Allow me.

285
00:15:25,421 --> 00:15:28,216
Good, next.

286
00:15:28,257 --> 00:15:35,139
[LESTRADE grunts] Your sleeves
are wet, open your hands.

287
00:15:35,181 --> 00:15:36,265
Show me your gloves!

288
00:15:36,307 --> 00:15:39,602
[CREWMAN 11F grunts]

289
00:15:46,568 --> 00:15:48,236
[TRANSPORT POLICE, CREW gasp]

290
00:15:48,277 --> 00:15:50,446
Red handed.

291
00:15:50,488 --> 00:15:53,366
Passenger Car Attendant
Eddie Hawthorne,

292
00:15:53,408 --> 00:15:55,243
you are the killer.

293
00:15:55,284 --> 00:15:57,245
Eddie? How could
you do such a thing?

294
00:15:57,286 --> 00:15:59,288
[CREWMAN 11D]
This is impossible!

295
00:15:59,330 --> 00:16:00,582
[CREWMAN 11A]
It can't be.

296
00:16:00,623 --> 00:16:02,124
Doctor Watson,

297
00:16:02,166 --> 00:16:04,085
it seems we owe you an apology.

298
00:16:04,126 --> 00:16:05,044
Please excuse us.

299
00:16:05,086 --> 00:16:06,921
You're mad! Me?

300
00:16:06,962 --> 00:16:08,590
Killing someone?

301
00:16:08,631 --> 00:16:12,761
Believe me. It's mine.
I had an accident!

302
00:16:12,802 --> 00:16:16,138
Prove it! Now take off
your gloves and show us!

303
00:16:16,180 --> 00:16:17,682
Of course.

304
00:16:23,646 --> 00:16:24,773
[LESTRADE]
What?

305
00:16:24,814 --> 00:16:28,109
While working, I cut myself
through the gloves.

306
00:16:28,150 --> 00:16:31,112
I was too busy to speak up.

307
00:16:32,363 --> 00:16:34,115
[CREWMAN 11F] Please,
you have to believe me!

308
00:16:34,156 --> 00:16:37,118
It's the truth! I'm innocent!

309
00:16:37,159 --> 00:16:40,789
That is, of course unless
you have some other proof?

310
00:16:42,749 --> 00:16:45,167
The man's
prevaricating, clearly.

311
00:16:45,209 --> 00:16:49,338
I agree. Still, nothing
else implicates him.

312
00:16:49,380 --> 00:16:52,341
Even after we recover the weapon
from the side of the tracks,

313
00:16:52,383 --> 00:16:55,052
he could claim it
doesn't belong to him.

314
00:16:55,094 --> 00:16:56,596
[WILLIAM]
This certainly is odd.

315
00:16:56,638 --> 00:16:57,931
[gasps]

316
00:17:01,267 --> 00:17:03,227
[HOLMES]
William?

317
00:17:03,269 --> 00:17:05,730
You told us you hurt yourself
through the gloves.

318
00:17:05,772 --> 00:17:06,731
Is that right?

319
00:17:06,773 --> 00:17:07,899
Well, yes.

320
00:17:07,941 --> 00:17:10,067
Then kindly explain...

321
00:17:10,109 --> 00:17:12,445
How did blood land
on your eyeglasses?

322
00:17:12,487 --> 00:17:14,906
[CREWMAN 11F gasping]

323
00:17:14,948 --> 00:17:16,825
I-- That is...

324
00:17:16,866 --> 00:17:18,075
Huh?

325
00:17:18,117 --> 00:17:20,077
[WILLIAM] Injuring your palms,
whilst wearing gloves,

326
00:17:20,119 --> 00:17:23,498
still doesn't explain how
blood got on the frames.

327
00:17:23,539 --> 00:17:24,916
Unless

328
00:17:24,958 --> 00:17:27,794
it landed there when you killed
Redwood in his compartment.

329
00:17:27,836 --> 00:17:29,712
[CREWMAN 11F] No- I.

330
00:17:29,754 --> 00:17:31,589
That's not the only clue.

331
00:17:31,631 --> 00:17:33,633
There are far more.

332
00:17:33,675 --> 00:17:37,762
If you would like,
I could point out each one.

333
00:17:37,804 --> 00:17:39,681
[CREWMAN 11F grunts]

334
00:17:39,722 --> 00:17:43,977
The manifest said
that Redwood was a jeweller.

335
00:17:44,018 --> 00:17:48,356
I never meant to kill the man.
I just meant to--

336
00:17:52,193 --> 00:17:53,611
[TRANSPORT POLICE 11A]
Eddie Hawthorne.

337
00:17:53,653 --> 00:17:56,030
You're under arrest for murder!

338
00:17:57,072 --> 00:17:58,282
Excellent.

339
00:17:58,324 --> 00:18:01,118
We should arrive at Grantham
in three minutes or so.

340
00:18:01,160 --> 00:18:04,956
Seems we made it in time,
Mister Holmes.

341
00:18:04,998 --> 00:18:06,749
Liam, you scoundrel.

342
00:18:06,791 --> 00:18:09,460
You're even better
at bluffing than I am.

343
00:18:09,502 --> 00:18:12,296
[WILLIAM] My dear sir,
whatever do you mean?

344
00:18:13,506 --> 00:18:16,009
[WILLIAM]
Well, we must take our leave.

345
00:18:16,050 --> 00:18:18,219
[HOLMES]
Let's grab a bite sometime.

346
00:18:20,346 --> 00:18:22,891
[LESTRADE]
Is this destiny?

347
00:18:22,932 --> 00:18:26,019
A man, equal in
intellect to Holmes,

348
00:18:26,060 --> 00:18:29,063
here as his contemporary.

349
00:18:29,104 --> 00:18:30,857
Moriarty.

350
00:18:37,112 --> 00:18:38,406
[LOUIS]
I'm impressed how you spotted

351
00:18:38,447 --> 00:18:40,742
a blood stain that
small, Brother.

352
00:18:40,783 --> 00:18:42,994
[WILLIAM] Well, I was
the one who put it there.

353
00:18:43,036 --> 00:18:44,328
What?

354
00:18:44,370 --> 00:18:47,081
I anticipated that the murderer
would try to be evasive.

355
00:18:48,290 --> 00:18:50,125
[WILLIAM]
So I intervened.

356
00:18:50,167 --> 00:18:51,836
Before Holmes
presented his case,

357
00:18:51,878 --> 00:18:53,629
I met with each crewmember,

358
00:18:53,671 --> 00:18:55,715
fabricating pretenses
to plant my blood on them

359
00:18:55,757 --> 00:18:57,800
in various places.

360
00:19:00,803 --> 00:19:02,221
[LOUIS]
If you hadn't been there,

361
00:19:02,263 --> 00:19:05,558
the case wouldn't have resolved
before we reached the station.

362
00:19:05,600 --> 00:19:09,520
So Holmes's endgame
isn't as strong as he thinks.

363
00:19:09,562 --> 00:19:10,939
[WILLIAM] His method
would have been enough to

364
00:19:10,980 --> 00:19:13,441
prove Hawthorne's
guilt at trial.

365
00:19:13,482 --> 00:19:16,778
Whereas my method freed
Doctor Watson before then.

366
00:19:18,112 --> 00:19:20,322
Holmes is far too trusting.

367
00:19:20,364 --> 00:19:23,576
However, that's not
necessarily such a bad trait.

368
00:19:23,618 --> 00:19:27,622
On the contrary, it's one
thing I like about him.

369
00:19:27,663 --> 00:19:31,125
Plus, those two, they make
a rather good pair.

370
00:19:42,011 --> 00:19:43,930
[WATSON]
Ironic, isn't it?

371
00:19:43,972 --> 00:19:47,433
I was the one who told you to go
ahead and become a murderer.

372
00:19:47,475 --> 00:19:49,393
But if you hadn't
been there for me

373
00:19:49,435 --> 00:19:52,855
undoubtedly, I would've
been the one branded a killer.

374
00:19:53,982 --> 00:19:57,526
Uh, John... I...

375
00:19:57,568 --> 00:20:00,446
Ah... So, look.

376
00:20:00,488 --> 00:20:03,157
Lately things haven't
been going my way.

377
00:20:03,198 --> 00:20:04,325
You know?

378
00:20:04,366 --> 00:20:06,160
But, as long as
you're okay with it

379
00:20:06,201 --> 00:20:08,955
then I wouldn't mind us
teaming up again.

380
00:20:10,581 --> 00:20:14,627
My word, is that really
your idea of an apology?

381
00:20:14,669 --> 00:20:15,878
[HOLMES grumbles]

382
00:20:15,920 --> 00:20:19,966
It would seem the great
detective is a mere mortal.

383
00:20:20,008 --> 00:20:22,593
While I'm not sure what on earth
that's supposed to mean,

384
00:20:22,635 --> 00:20:25,554
tell me, are we
a team again or aren't we?

385
00:20:25,596 --> 00:20:28,016
[WATSON] Give me one of
your cigarettes, Sherlock.

386
00:20:28,057 --> 00:20:31,894
As for the matches,
I can handle those.

387
00:20:31,936 --> 00:20:34,939
I wouldn't be a good doctor if
I couldn't light a cigarette,

388
00:20:34,981 --> 00:20:36,440
wouldn't you say?

389
00:20:36,482 --> 00:20:40,235
[HOLMES laughs]
Doctor John Watson?

390
00:20:40,277 --> 00:20:41,821
Making a joke?

391
00:20:41,863 --> 00:20:45,700
For this to work you're
going to need a sense of humor!

392
00:20:45,741 --> 00:20:48,703
[HOLMES laughs]

393
00:21:20,401 --> 00:21:22,403
[MYCROFT]
Begging your pardon...

394
00:21:23,696 --> 00:21:26,574
...but I am here
to make my report,

395
00:21:26,615 --> 00:21:28,576
Your Majesty.

396
00:21:30,661 --> 00:21:33,497
I look forward to hearing it.

397
00:21:33,539 --> 00:21:36,291
As of now,
the situation is dire.

398
00:21:36,333 --> 00:21:38,627
Unfortunately, regarding
the stolen documents,

399
00:21:38,669 --> 00:21:41,338
the whereabouts remain unknown.

400
00:21:41,380 --> 00:21:43,091
Whoever has them
in their possession,

401
00:21:43,132 --> 00:21:46,010
they have yet to make
contact or list demands.

402
00:21:47,845 --> 00:21:50,264
That is regretful,
considering they hold

403
00:21:50,305 --> 00:21:53,350
one of Great Britain's
best kept secrets.

404
00:21:53,392 --> 00:21:57,980
You're free to use whatever
means, no matter how ruthless.

405
00:21:58,022 --> 00:22:04,612
Just retrieve those documents,
Sir Mycroft Holmes.

406
00:22:06,072 --> 00:22:09,283
Yes, Your Majesty.
